  <section>
    <h2>What happens to your remaining days after cancelling?</h2>
    <p>Turning off auto-renewal does not stop Premium <strong>immediately</strong>. It only <strong>prevents the next charge</strong>. The period you've already paid for (say, through the end of the month) plays out in full — 4 GB uploads, custom emoji, the animated avatar, all of it stays until the term ends.</p>
    <p>What happens once that term actually ends is covered in detail in <a href="/en/blog/telegram-premium-expired">Telegram Premium expired: what you lose and how to renew</a> — the short version: your account and messages are untouched, only the Premium features stop.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Do you get a partial refund for cancelling early?</h2>
    <p>No. Under standard App Store and Google Play terms, cancelling in the middle of a billing period does <strong>not</strong> produce a partial refund — the amount you paid is treated as covering the entire period, with no credit for unused days. That makes it financially sensible to cancel closer to the end of a term, once you've actually used what you paid for.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What to do if the cancel button is missing or greyed out</h2>
    <p>This is the most common snag. The usual causes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You switched devices.</strong> Subscribed on Android, then moved to an iPhone? The subscription is still managed by Google Play. Fix: sign into that same Google account on any device or browser via play.google.com/store/account and cancel there.</li>
      <li><strong>Desktop/Web limitation.</strong> Telegram Desktop or Web simply doesn't display a cancel button for a subscription bought on mobile. Cancellation only works on the device or platform where the payment actually happened.</li>
      <li><strong>Premium was a gift.</strong> If someone else gifted you Premium, the subscription (if any) is managed by whoever bought it, not by you.</li>
      <li><strong>Bought through a reseller bot.</strong> Premium bought through bots like StarsJoy isn't a subscription at all — it's a one-time activation, so there's no cancel button because there's nothing recurring to cancel.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

